 ### 1 — The process dies after the write and before any sync
 
-<What is on the device, what recovery sees, which policies lose what here, and what the operator
-observes. The first two points on this page differ only in *which* thing died, and the difference is
-the whole point of the page.>
+`kill -9`, a panic in an unrelated task, an OOM kill, a container that is stopped mid-connection.
+
+- **On the device:** nothing of the records written since the last flush — but that is the wrong place
+  to look. The bytes are in the kernel's page cache, the file's length includes them, and the kernel
+  owns them until it decides to write them out or the machine stops.
+- **Recovery sees:** every record the store acknowledged, in order, and nothing of a frame that was
+  half received. The kernel outlived the process, so the page cache is intact.
+- **Who loses what:** nobody. `per-record`, `interval(50ms)` and `never` all lose nothing here, because
+  nothing was lost: the page cache is not the write path's to lose. This is the crash that teaches the
+  difference between the two words above, and the one that makes "we fsync so that a crash cannot lose
+  data" a sentence about *which* crash.
+- **Operator sees:** a process that starts, recovers a whole log, and serves; no data re-sent by any
+  client. The interesting question is what the process was doing, not what it lost.
 
 ### 2 — The machine dies after the write and before any sync
 
-<Same four questions. Name the policies and the bound each one puts on the loss.>
+Power loss, a kernel panic, a hypervisor that takes the VM away, a `reboot -f`.
+
+- **On the device:** every record durable as of the last successful flush, and nothing else. The page
+  cache is gone with the machine, so anything written and not flushed is gone too — including records
+  whose clients were already answered.
+- **Recovery sees:** a prefix of the log ending at the last flush. Whether the *file* holds more than
+  that depends on the device's own write cache, which is why a torn tail (point 3) is the next point
+  and not an afterthought.
+- **Who loses what:** `per-record` loses nothing — it flushed before it answered. `interval(50ms)`
+  loses the acknowledged writes of the last window, at most 50 ms of them. `never` loses everything
+  written since the last flush the *operating system* made on its own schedule, which nobody can
+  bound: it might be 30 seconds, it might be several minutes on an idle machine.
+- **Operator sees:** a store that starts with fewer records than its clients sent, and no way to tell
+  the difference between "the last 50 ms are missing" and "the store is broken" without this page. The
+  clients that lost data have to be found and told, which is the cost of the policies that lose it.
 
 ### 3 — The machine dies during a write (a torn record)
 
-<Same four questions. Say what the last record looks like on the device, and what a reader can and
-cannot tell about it without a checksum.>
+The power goes while a record's bytes are being written, so the file ends in the middle of one: the
+length prefix is there and the payload is short, or the payload is there and half of it is another
+value.
+
+- **On the device:** a record whose tail is whatever the device managed to commit — a prefix of the
+  record, or garbage where the rest of it should be. The device's own write cache decides how much of
+  it survived, and a device that lies about its cache decides it wrongly.
+- **Recovery sees:** something that is *not a record* on the last line: a length that promises more
+  bytes than the file holds, or a payload whose checksum does not match. `Spool::records` refuses the
+  file today (it answers `Error::Protocol` rather than guessing); `lakelet/4.4-wal-recovery` is the
+  step that replays up to the last whole record and says what it dropped instead of refusing the
+  store.
+- **Who loses what:** all three policies lose the torn record itself — it was never acknowledged
+  unless a flush had already covered it, and `durable_offset` never moved past it. The difference is
+  the *neighbourhood*: `per-record` has at most one such record, `interval(50ms)` has the window's
+  worth, and `never` has everything since the last flush. The bytes that come back wrong are only ever
+  visible *after* the truncation point.
+- **Operator sees:** a store that refuses to start (`protocol error: record 41 says 37 byte(s) and the
+  file holds 12 after its header`), which is alarming, correct, and temporary until 4.4 teaches the
+  log to end at the last whole record.
 
 ### 4 — The file's data is durable but its directory entry is not
 
-<Same four questions. Say which write makes a store *exist* for a reader, and which one does not.>
+The store directory was created, the file inside it was created, bytes were written and flushed — and
+the *directory* was never flushed. A machine death between those two facts takes the file's name with
+it.
+
+- **On the device:** the file's data blocks, with no directory entry that names them: from any reader's
+  point of view the store is not there at all, and the space it occupies is reclaimed as the filesystem
+  sees fit.
+- **Recovery sees:** nothing at all. There is no file to replay on a filesystem where the entry was
+  lost — the failure is not a missing tail but a missing store, which is why it is worse than every
+  other point on this page.
+- **Who loses what:** all of them, and it is the one point on this page where the *choice of policy*
+  does not matter even a little: `per-record`, `interval(50ms)` and `never` are equally gone. Data that
+  was flushed is not data that was named.
+- **Operator sees:** an empty store directory on a machine that was serving a moment ago, with the
+  data unrecoverable and the flush that "succeeded" having nothing to point at. The store pays this
+  once, when it creates the file: `Spool::with_policy` flushes the store directory after creating the
+  file, so this point is a fixed cost at startup rather than a window that stays open.
 
 ### 5 — The flush itself fails (the device, not the store)
 
-<Same four questions. Say what "acknowledged" turns into when the device answers a flush with an error,
-and what the store has to stop claiming.>
+The device reports an error when it is asked to flush: a failing disk, a full filesystem, an
+unreachable network volume, a filesystem that has gone read-only on its own.
+
+- **On the device:** unknown, and that is the point. The bytes may be there, they may not, and the
+  store has no way to find out — a failed flush is not "no answer", it is "no answer that can be
+  trusted".
+- **Recovery sees:** whatever the device actually kept, which may be less than the log's length and
+  more than the last flush. A record that was written but not flushed may or may not be there, and its
+  *contents* may be a prefix.
+- **Who loses what:** every policy is in the same position, and the one that *looked* safest is the
+  most affected: `per-record` answered a client and then failed to flush, so it has told somebody
+  "stored" about a record it can no longer vouch for; `interval(50ms)` and `never` at least never
+  claimed the window was closed. The honest sentence for all three is that `sync` returned an error
+  and the boundary did not move: `durable_offset` still names the last flush that succeeded, and a
+  client's acknowledged records above it are in doubt.
+- **Operator sees:** a store error on the write path (`store error: ...`) and `durable_offset` that has
+  stopped advancing while `len` keeps growing — the two numbers are the diagnosis. The response is not
+  a retry loop: it is stopping the write path, because a store that cannot flush cannot keep accepting,
+  and every acknowledgment after that point is a claim nobody can check.
 
 ### 6 — The process stops cleanly (SIGTERM, and what "clean" is worth)
 
-<Same four questions. A clean stop is not a crash — say what it does and does not put on the device,
-and which window survives into the reboot that follows it.>
